--  Tasks and protected objects: doing two things at once, and sharing what
--  they touch.
--
--  Read the design note first, because it decides what these mean here.
--
--  Adash already had a concurrency model: *processes*. `start`, `pipe` and the
--  job commands run programs beside the shell, and that is what a shell is
--  for. Ada's tasks are a second model, and two models answering one question
--  is a cost this language pays deliberately -- the reason to pay it is that a
--  submission is a *program*, and a program that wants to overlap two pieces
--  of its own work had no way to say so.
--
--  Tasks here are interleaved, not parallel. The machine runs one strand at a
--  time and changes strand at defined points. Ada does not require parallelism
--  -- a single-processor implementation is conforming -- and what a program
--  may rely on is that its tasks make progress and that its synchronisation
--  holds. Interleaving is also the only answer this repository can give:
--  anything platform-specific belongs to hostkit, and a machine reaching for
--  threads of its own would be a second provider of them.

--  `pragma Detect_Blocking;` asks for an operation that may wait, run inside a
--  protected operation, to be caught. A strand set aside while it holds a lock
--  is holding it against everybody, which is deadlock waiting to be written --
--  and Ada makes it a pragma rather than a rule because the check costs
--  something on every instruction.
--
--  Everything below is written to pass it: what a protected object waits on
--  here is its own barriers, which give the lock up to wait.
pragma Detect_Blocking;

--  A dispatching policy says what a strand's turn is. Two are accepted, and
--  each is a claim about how the program runs rather than a switch that might
--  mean nothing: `FIFO_Within_Priorities`, which is Ravenscar's and means a
--  strand keeps its turn until it waits for something, and
--  `Round_Robin_Within_Priorities`, which is what the machine does when
--  nobody says -- a turn of a fixed number of instructions, shared out
--  between strands of the same priority. Anything else is refused rather than
--  accepted and ignored.
--
--  This file names the second, and the reason is worth the space. Further
--  down it writes a long computation that waits for nothing, to show a
--  timeout abandoning work. Under Ravenscar's policy that computation would
--  keep the machine until it finished and the timeout would never fire --
--  which is not a defect in either but Ada's own cost, on one processor, for
--  an interleaving nobody slices. A program written for Ravenscar does not
--  contain that loop. This one does, deliberately, so it says so.
pragma Task_Dispatching_Policy (Round_Robin_Within_Priorities);

--  `pragma Priority_Specific_Dispatching (P, First, Last);` gives a policy to
--  a range of priorities instead of to the whole program -- a program can
--  leave the strands doing its important work alone and share the rest out.
--  It is not written here because the pragma above already answers for every
--  priority, and a priority that two pragmas answer for is one the reader has
--  to guess about, so the second is refused rather than quietly winning.

--  `pragma Queuing_Policy (Priority_Queuing);` takes the highest-priority
--  caller off an entry queue first rather than the one that arrived first,
--  and among callers of equal priority still the one that arrived first --
--  which is what makes it an ordering rather than a lottery. It reaches into
--  a selective accept too: the alternative served is the one whose queue holds
--  that caller, rather than the one written first. `FIFO_Queuing` is the
--  other, and is what the machine does when nobody says -- and there the
--  alternative written first is the one served, which Ada leaves arbitrary.
--  Not written here, because what this file shows about queues is that
--  callers are taken in the order they arrived.
--
--  `Ceiling_Locking` is what the machine already does, and saying so costs
--  nothing: a caller above an object's ceiling is refused, and no strand is
--  set aside inside a protected operation. It is the only locking policy
--  here, because it is the only one this machine could be told about and mean
--  it.
pragma Locking_Policy (Ceiling_Locking);

--  `pragma Restrictions (No_Abort_Statements);` is a program saying what it
--  will not do, so that a reader can rely on it -- worth more than a comment,
--  because the analyser holds the program to it wherever the pragma stands.
--  The rest are most of Ada's Ravenscar profile: `No_Delay`,
--  `No_Relative_Delay`, `No_Select_Statements`, `No_Requeue_Statements`,
--  `No_Dynamic_Priorities`, `No_Task_Hierarchy` and
--  `No_Local_Protected_Objects` -- every task and object declared where the
--  program begins -- `Simple_Barriers`, `No_Task_Termination`, and the counted
--  ones: `Max_Tasks`, `Max_Task_Entries`, `Max_Protected_Entries` and
--  `Max_Entry_Queue_Length`.
--
--  Ravenscar's point is that a program which gives all of this up can be
--  reasoned about: what runs, what it waits for and how much of it there is
--  are settled where the program begins.
--
--  `pragma Profile (Ravenscar);` is the name for all of it at once -- the
--  restrictions above, the blocking check, and both policies. A program that
--  names it and a program that writes them out run the same way, which is the
--  only thing a profile can honestly be.
--
--  `pragma Profile (Jorvik);` is Ada's other one: Ravenscar with four things
--  given back -- a barrier worked out rather than read, more than one entry
--  on a protected object, more than one caller queued at one, and a delay for
--  a length. Each of the four was given up for a reason about *analysis*
--  rather than about safety, and Jorvik is Ada saying that a program willing
--  to pay for the analysis may have them back.
--
--  What it costs is worth knowing before writing it: `No_Task_Termination`
--  means a task that ends is an error, and Ravenscar means that because its
--  programs do not end. A submission does. So a Ravenscar submission finishes
--  by reporting the task that ran out, and the profile is written here for
--  the language it is, not because a shell is where such a program belongs.
--
--  This file writes one of each of the things Ravenscar gives up, which is
--  what it is for, so it names none of them and could not name the profile.

--  A protected object is how two tasks share something. Its operations run
--  with mutual exclusion, which on an interleaving machine is not an
--  optimisation on top of something else: the machine simply does not change
--  strand while one is running.
--  What a family of entries is indexed by: a discrete type, because a family
--  is a run of entries and a run is counted.
type Milestone is (Half_Way, All_Of_It);

protected Tally is
   --  What a task may be and still call it: its ceiling. The watcher below
   --  runs at 20, and an object it calls has to admit callers that high --
   --  which is the whole of what a ceiling is for, and what a program that
   --  gets it wrong is told about rather than left to assume.
   pragma Priority (25);

   procedure Add (This_Many : Integer);
   function Total return Integer;

   --  An entry is a barrier and a body -- a way to wait until something
   --  holds. A caller that finds the barrier closed is set aside, and the
   --  barrier is asked again when the object is next left.
   entry Wait_For_Enough;

   --  A family of them: one entry per value, each with a queue of its own.
   --  Its body is one body for all of them and says what to call the member it
   --  is running for -- a barrier that could not ask which member it was would
   --  make a family no different from one entry.
   entry Wait_For (Milestone);
end Tally;

protected body Tally is
   Running : Integer := 0;

   procedure Add (This_Many : Integer) is
   begin
      Running := Running + This_Many;
   end Add;

   function Total return Integer is
   begin
      return Running;
   end Total;

   entry Wait_For_Enough when Running >= 100 is
   begin
      put_line ("enough: " & Trim (Running'Image));
   end Wait_For_Enough;

   entry Wait_For (for Which in Milestone)
     when Running >= Milestone'Pos (Which) * 50 is
   begin
      put_line ("reached " & Milestone'Image (Which));
   end Wait_For;
end Tally;

--  A protected *type* is a protected object worth having more than one of.
--  Each object has state and a lock of its own, and an object of one is made
--  by copying the type's declaration and body with the type's name replaced by
--  the object's -- which is what a generic instantiation here already does.
--  What it costs is a copy of the code per object; what it buys is that
--  everything below the analyser sees what it has always seen.
--  And it takes what it needs at elaboration. A discriminant of a protected
--  type arrives as a constant at the head of the object's own body, which is
--  what a discriminant is: something the type is written against and the
--  object fixes.
protected type Tally_Sheet (Called : String := "a tally sheet") is
   procedure Add (This_Many : Integer);
   function Total return Integer;
   procedure Say_It;
end Tally_Sheet;

protected body Tally_Sheet is
   Running : Integer := 0;

   procedure Say_It is
   begin
      put_line (Called & " has " & Trim (Running'Image));
   end Say_It;

   procedure Add (This_Many : Integer) is
   begin
      Running := Running + This_Many;
   end Add;

   function Total return Integer is
   begin
      return Running;
   end Total;
end Tally_Sheet;

--  A type whose discriminants all default is one an object may stand without
--  a constraint on. All of them or none of them: a constraint constrains the
--  whole of a type, and a partial one would leave a program saying which ones
--  it meant to leave out by counting.
Morning   : Tally_Sheet ("the morning");
Afternoon : Tally_Sheet;

Morning.Add (3);
Morning.Add (4);
Afternoon.Add (10);

Morning.Say_It;
Afternoon.Say_It;

--  A task runs beside what declared it. `task T;` declares one with no
--  entries, which is the commonest shape a script wants: something that gets
--  on with a piece of work.
--  What a task runs at. A strand of higher priority is preferred whenever the
--  machine chooses which runs next -- chosen, not pre-empted: this machine
--  changes strand at defined points, so a priority decides who goes next
--  rather than interrupting who is going.
--
--  `X'Priority := N;` changes it while the program runs, and reading it back
--  asks the machine rather than the declaration: either a task's priority or a
--  protected object's ceiling may have moved since.
task Watcher is
   pragma Priority (20);
end Watcher;

task Counter_One;
task Counter_Two;

task body Counter_One is
begin
   for Step in 1 .. 40 loop
      Tally.Add (1);
   end loop;
end Counter_One;

task body Counter_Two is
begin
   for Step in 1 .. 60 loop
      Tally.Add (1);
   end loop;
end Counter_Two;

--  This one blocks until the others have done enough between them. Which of
--  the three gets there first is the scheduler's business; that this one does
--  not pass until the barrier holds is the language's.
task body Watcher is
begin
   Tally.Wait_For (Half_Way);
   Tally.Wait_For_Enough;
end Watcher;

--  `delay` waits in real time, from Ada's own monotonic clock -- monotonic
--  rather than calendar, so a script that waits half a second waits half a
--  second whatever somebody does to the system time. The other strands run
--  while one waits, and when none can the machine sleeps rather than spins.
--
--  `Clock` reads that clock, and `delay until` waits for a time on it rather
--  than for a length. Ada has both because a loop that delays *for* a tenth of
--  a second drifts by however long its own body takes, and one that delays
--  *until* the next tenth does not.
task Slow_Starter;
task body Slow_Starter is
begin
   delay 0.05;
   Tally.Add (0);
end Slow_Starter;

--  `select ... else ...` is Ada's conditional entry call: take the entry if its
--  barrier is open *now*, and do something else if it is not. The lock is taken
--  before the barrier is asked and nothing changes strand while it is held, so
--  the barrier cannot close between being asked and being acted on -- which is
--  the whole difficulty of writing one.
select
   Tally.Wait_For_Enough;
   put_line ("already enough");
else
   put_line ("not yet");
end select;

--  `or delay D` bounds how long the call waits, and the call waits the way an
--  ordinary one does: a caller of a protected entry queues by being inside the
--  entry's own body, parked at its barrier, so what the deadline reaches is
--  that parking. A barrier that opens during the wait is taken when it opens,
--  and one that does not is given up on -- the caller leaves the queue, and
--  what it was going to be given is not given.
select
   Tally.Wait_For_Enough;
   put_line ("got there");
or delay 0.4;
   put_line ("still not enough");
end select;


--  A rendezvous is how two tasks meet. An entry of a *task* is not a barrier
--  and a body -- that is a protected object's -- but a place two strands come
--  to: `Ledger.Record_It (7);` on one side and `accept Record_It ... do` on the
--  other are one event, and neither side passes it until both have arrived.
--
--  That is what a rendezvous gives that leaving a value somewhere does not:
--  the caller knows the body ran, and the body knows the caller is still there
--  to be handed something back.
--  An entry *family* is a run of entries rather than one: `entry E (T)`
--  declares one per value of T, and a caller says which by writing `E (V)`.
--  What it is worth is that an acceptor can serve one member and leave the
--  rest queued, so a task can decide what to serve rather than only when.
type Urgency is (Whenever, Right_Now);

task Ledger is
   entry Take_It (Urgency);
   entry Record_It (Amount : Integer);
   entry Total (So_Far : out Integer);

   --  Where a caller is put when the ledger is not ready for it yet.
   entry Wait_For_Room;
end Ledger;

task body Ledger is
   Running : Integer := 0;

   --  What a caller gave before it was moved to another queue, kept because
   --  the queue it was moved to takes nothing.
   Pending : Integer := 0;
begin
   --  Written again as Ada writes it, and held to the profile it was declared
   --  with: the caller writes its arguments by the entry's profile and this
   --  body reads them by this one, so two that disagreed would have one side
   --  writing a number where the other reads text.
   --  An accept body is a master too: what it starts is finished with before
   --  the caller is let go, which is what a caller is entitled to assume of a
   --  rendezvous. This language accepts a declaration wherever a statement may
   --  stand, so there is something for that rule to be about -- and the total
   --  the next caller reads is right because of it.
   --  Served in the order the task chooses, not the order they arrived:
   --  the urgent one first, whichever queued first.
   accept Take_It (Right_Now) do
      put_line ("took the urgent one first");
   end Take_It;

   --  `requeue` says "not this one, that one": the caller being served is put
   --  on another entry's queue and this body is left. The caller does not
   --  resume and is not told -- from its own point of view it is still waiting
   --  for the call it made, which is what makes a requeue different from
   --  returning and being called again.
   --
   --  What the target takes is nothing, so a body that needs the caller's
   --  argument takes it before moving the caller on. Nothing after the requeue
   --  in this body runs: leaving it is what a requeue does.
   --
   --  `requeue E with abort;` is the other spelling, and it decides whether
   --  the caller keeps its way out: without it the call becomes uncancellable,
   --  so a deadline a timed call was made with stops applying and an abandoned
   --  trigger can no longer pull it out of the queue. The caller never asked to
   --  be where it now is, which is why the body doing the moving is the one
   --  that says. Written without it here, because these callers have no way
   --  out to keep -- both made plain calls and neither is bounded.
   --  Either end of a requeue may be a member of a family: which member is
   --  something the body works out, on the side it was taken from as much as
   --  on the side it goes to.
   accept Take_It (Whenever) do
      requeue Take_It (Right_Now);
   end Take_It;

   accept Take_It (Right_Now) do
      put_line ("the patient one was moved up and served");
   end Take_It;

   accept Record_It (Amount : Integer) do
      Pending := Amount;
      requeue Wait_For_Room;
   end Record_It;

   accept Wait_For_Room do
      put_line ("the first one waited for room");
      Running := Running + Pending;
   end Wait_For_Room;

   accept Record_It (Amount : Integer) do
      task Adding;
      task body Adding is
      begin
         Running := Running + Amount;
      end Adding;
   end Record_It;

   --  An `out` parameter is how a task hands something back. The arguments
   --  live in a run of the *caller's* slots and these formals are references
   --  into it, so writing here writes where the caller is reading from --
   --  which is the only arrangement that works when the two sides are separate
   --  strands with frames of their own.
   --  `E'Count` is how many callers are queued at an entry. Asked inside the
   --  body that declares it, which is Ada's rule and not fussiness: a count is
   --  a queue's length at an instant, and a caller reading it from outside
   --  would be told something that had already changed by the time it acted.
   accept Total (So_Far : out Integer) do
      put_line ("still waiting to record: " & Trim (Record_It'Count'Image));
      So_Far := Running;
   end Total;
end Ledger;

--  Two callers, the patient one first. Which is served first is the task's
--  choice, and it chooses by naming a member.
task Patient_One;
task body Patient_One is
begin
   Ledger.Take_It (Whenever);
end Patient_One;

for Step in 1 .. 3000 loop null; end loop;
Ledger.Take_It (Right_Now);

Ledger.Record_It (30);
Ledger.Record_It (12);

Counted : Integer := 0;
Ledger.Total (Counted);

put_line ("the ledger says " & Trim (Counted'Image));

--  `or delay D` bounds a call to a *task* entry too, and underneath it is a
--  different thing. A protected entry has a barrier to look at, and the timed
--  call above looks at it; a task entry has nobody to ask, so the call joins
--  the queue carrying a deadline and leaves it again if no rendezvous has
--  begun by then. Ada cancels a call that has not *started*, which is why one
--  begun in time runs to its end however long the body takes -- and why the
--  acceptor that arrives late finds the queue empty.
--
--  `else` instead of `or delay D` does not wait at all: the call is made only
--  if a rendezvous can start at once, and otherwise the else part runs. What
--  answers is the task called -- one waiting at an accept has written down
--  what it would take, and one doing anything else is waiting for nobody. Its
--  guards count, so a closed alternative is nobody waiting.
--
--  The doorman below is still asleep when the second knock comes, so the
--  second knock does not wait. Had it been at the accept, the call would have
--  been made and the rendezvous run to its end.
task Doorman is
   entry Knock;
end Doorman;

task body Doorman is
begin
   --  Late on purpose, so that the caller below runs out of patience first.
   delay 0.15;

   --  Long enough that what it says comes after everything else this file
   --  prints, on a loaded machine as well as an idle one. An example is a
   --  test here, and an order that holds only when the host is quick is an
   --  expectation nobody can rely on.
   select
      accept Knock do
         put_line ("the doorman answered");
      end Knock;
   or delay 0.60;
      put_line ("the doorman found nobody, and the queue holds"
                & Knock'Count'Image);
   end select;
end Doorman;

select
   Doorman.Knock;
   put_line ("knocked and was let in");
or delay 0.04;
   put_line ("knocked and gave up");
end select;

select
   Doorman.Knock;
   put_line ("knocked again and was let in");
else
   put_line ("knocked again, and nobody was at the door");
end select;

--  A task type takes what it needs at elaboration. Discriminants are to a task
--  what parameters are to a subprogram -- given where the object is declared,
--  constant inside the body -- and they are what makes two objects of one type
--  different rather than merely separate.
task type Timer (Seconds : Float; Called : String) is
   entry Stand_Down;
end Timer;

task body Timer is
begin
   select
      accept Stand_Down;
   or delay Seconds;
      put_line (Called & " fired");
   end select;
end Timer;

Impatient : Timer (0.05, "the impatient one");
Patient   : Timer (30.0, "the patient one");

Patient.Stand_Down;

--  A task *type* is a task worth having more than one of. Its objects are each
--  a task of their own -- their own strand, their own local state, their own
--  entry queues -- and that is why a task is a type at all: several tasks share
--  one body, so what a rendezvous names has to be the object rather than the
--  work. A task object holds which strand runs it, and that is the whole of it.
task type Adder is
   entry Take (Amount : Integer);
   entry Total (So_Far : out Integer);
end Adder;

task body Adder is
   Running : Integer := 0;
begin
   --  `select` with several alternatives is a task saying "whichever of these
   --  happens first". Its alternatives are accepts, where the other `select`
   --  has an entry call: one is a task deciding what to serve and the other is
   --  a caller deciding how long to wait.
   --
   --  A guard -- `when <condition> =>` -- says what this task is ready for.
   --  Guards are asked once, when the select is reached, which is Ada's rule:
   --  an alternative that was closed then stays closed for this execution
   --  however the world changes while it waits.
   --
   --  `or delay D;` beside these alternatives bounds the waiting rather than
   --  replacing it: such a select waits for a caller *or* the clock, and a
   --  caller arriving is served as it arrives rather than when the wait runs
   --  out. A task in one is waiting for somebody, which is also what a
   --  conditional call to it is told.
   for Times in 1 .. 3 loop
      select
         accept Take (Amount : Integer) do
            Running := Running + Amount;
         end Take;
      or
         when Running > 0 =>
            accept Total (So_Far : out Integer) do
               So_Far := Running;
            end Total;
      end select;
   end loop;
end Adder;

Left  : Adder;
Right : Adder;

Left.Take (2);
Left.Take (3);
Right.Take (30);
Right.Take (7);

Left_Says  : Integer := 0;
Right_Says : Integer := 0;

Left.Total (Left_Says);
Right.Total (Right_Says);

put_line ("left " & Trim (Left_Says'Image)
          & ", right " & Trim (Right_Says'Image));

--  `select ... then abort` abandons work when something else happens first.
--  The abortable part runs as a strand of its own and the trigger waits in
--  this one, which is the shape this machine can give it and a faithful one: a
--  strand that is not running cannot be in the middle of anything, so there is
--  nothing to unwind when it is abandoned.
--
--  Either side may win. When the trigger fires the work is abandoned; when the
--  work finishes first the trigger is cancelled and its statements do not run
--  -- and the wait ends there rather than running its full course.
--
--  The trigger waits longer than anything else in this file, so that what it
--  prints comes after the timers above however loaded the machine is. An
--  example is a test here, and two waits of the same length are a coin toss
--  rather than an expectation.
Steps_Taken : Integer := 0;

select
   delay 0.20;
   put_line ("the long way round ran out of time");
then abort
   for Step in 1 .. 10000000 loop
      Steps_Taken := Steps_Taken + 1;
   end loop;

   put_line ("the long way round finished");
end select;

select
   delay 30.0;
   put_line ("the short way round ran out of time");
then abort
   put_line ("the short way round finished");
end select;

put_line ("started");

--  A master is a *region*, not a program. A subprogram does not return while a
--  task declared inside it is still running, and neither does a task body:
--  what a task reads through its static link is the frame it was declared in,
--  and a region that left first would leave it reading slots the next call had
--  taken.
Steps_Run : Integer := 0;

procedure Do_It_Twice is
   task Once;
   task Again;

   task body Once is
   begin
      Steps_Run := Steps_Run + 1;
   end Once;

   task body Again is
   begin
      Steps_Run := Steps_Run + 1;
   end Again;
begin
   null;
end Do_It_Twice;

Do_It_Twice;
Do_It_Twice;

--  Four, and read straight after the call rather than at the end of the
--  script: each call waited for the two tasks it started, and the second call
--  waited for its own rather than for the first call's.
put_line ("steps run " & Trim (Steps_Run'Image));

--  A block statement is a master too, which is Ada's rule and the useful one:
--  it is how a script says "start these, and do not go on until they are
--  done" without writing a subprogram to hold them.
--
--  A block makes no frame, so what says which of them a task belongs to is a
--  numbered region rather than a frame -- numbered rather than counted,
--  because a depth would make the second turn of a loop look like the first.
declare
   task Left_Half;
   task Right_Half;

   task body Left_Half is
   begin
      Steps_Run := Steps_Run + 10;
   end Left_Half;

   task body Right_Half is
   begin
      Steps_Run := Steps_Run + 100;
   end Right_Half;
begin
   null;
end;

put_line ("and after the block " & Trim (Steps_Run'Image));

--  A task answers two questions about itself. They are not each other's
--  negation: a task that has run its body out and is still waiting for what
--  depends on it has *completed* without having *terminated*, and answers False
--  to both -- it has ended nothing and will meet nobody.
task Asked_About;
task body Asked_About is
begin
   null;
end Asked_About;

put_line ("while it may still be running: "
          & Boolean'Image (Asked_About'Terminated)
          & " " & Boolean'Image (Asked_About'Callable));

--  And how long it has had turns for, which is not how long it has existed: a
--  task that spends its life at a barrier has used almost none of it.
put_line ("it has run for under a second: "
          & Boolean'Image (Asked_About'Execution_Time < 1.0));

--  `A'Identity` is which task, as something a program can hold. A task itself
--  cannot be copied -- what one *is* is the thing that runs, and a second name
--  for it would be a second of it -- so an identity has a type of its own, and
--  it answers the same two questions the task does.
Which_One : Task_Id := Asked_About'Identity;

put_line ("and the identity says the same: "
          & Boolean'Image (Which_One'Terminated)
          & " " & Boolean'Image (Which_One = Asked_About'Identity));

--  How much room something takes, in slots -- which is this machine's unit.
--  Ada counts bits for `'Size` and a host's bytes for `'Storage_Size`; a slot
--  here holds a value of any type, so either count would be a number nothing
--  in this machine means, and a plausible wrong answer is worse than an honest
--  different one.
put_line ("a tally sheet takes " & Trim (Morning'Size'Image)
          & " slots, and a task gets "
          & Trim (Asked_About'Storage_Size'Image));

--  And here is the rule that makes tasks usable in a script at all: the
--  declarative region that declared a task does not finish until the task has.
--  A submission is that region too, so the script waits -- which is why the
--  line after this one can read the total and see all of it.
--
--  One consequence worth knowing: `quit` ends the session, and a session that
--  has ended is not waiting for anything. A task still running when `quit` is
--  written does not finish.
--
--  A rendezvous that fails is the caller's business too. Ada re-raises at the
--  point of the entry call what the accept body failed with and releases the
--  caller -- it asked for a piece of work and is owed the answer, and being
--  left queued for ever is not one. The exception is carried to it and raised
--  where it resumes, because what is set aside cannot raise.
--
--  `abort T;` is the other way a task stops. It takes effect where the strand
--  would next have run, which is the next switch point: this machine
--  interleaves rather than pre-empts, so there is no moment between two
--  instructions at which to intervene -- and none is needed, because a strand
--  that is not running cannot be in the middle of anything. An aborted task
--  does not run its handlers, which is Ada's rule too.
--
--  `abort T, U;` names several, and they stop together rather than one after
--  another. What makes that more than tidiness is who is watching: a caller
--  queued at an aborted task is handed Tasking_Error, and being handed it
--  *resumes* the caller -- so telling one between two aborts would let it run
--  while a task the same statement names is still going.

--  `or terminate;` is how a server task ends. It serves callers for as long
--  as there are any, and when the master it depends on has finished and
--  nobody is left who could call it, it ends instead of waiting for a caller
--  who will never come. Without it, the loop below would be a task waiting
--  for something no task will do -- which is what this machine reports, and
--  reporting it is not the same as a program that ends.
--
--  Nothing the task itself does decides this: the alternative is taken when
--  every task that depends on the same master is either over or waiting at
--  one of these. So two servers that could call each other end together and
--  neither ends alone, and one task still sleeping keeps all of them alive.
task Tally_Keeper is
   entry Add (Amount : Integer);
end Tally_Keeper;

task body Tally_Keeper is
   Running : Integer := 0;
begin
   loop
      select
         accept Add (Amount : Integer) do
            Running := Running + Amount;
            put_line ("the keeper has" & Running'Image);
         end Add;
      or
         terminate;
      end select;
   end loop;
end Tally_Keeper;

Tally_Keeper.Add (2);
Tally_Keeper.Add (40);
